<section xml:id="sec-graphing-linear-inequalities" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Graphing Linear Inequalities</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.0 Numbers and Algebra
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.4 Linear Programming
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p component="core">
                        Represent linear inequalities on a cartesian plane
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        Draw linear inequalities on a cartesian plane
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        2 lessons of 40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Graphing linear inequalities on a Cartesian plane and identifying the feasible region.
         Suggested activity: Learners plot the inequalities from the fundraiser problem on graph paper and shade the unwanted region.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Sliders change the coefficients of two or three linear inequalities; the unwanted regions shade automatically and the feasible region is outlined. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core (b) represent, Essential (b) draw. Lessons 2.1 and 2.2 as subsections.
         Activity: as planning notes (plot the fundraiser inequalities and shade the unwanted region); lesson 2.2 finds its corners
         JSXGraph: fig-lp-feasible-region (planning notes: sliders on three inequalities, unwanted sides shaded, R outlined; assets/jsxgraph/lp-feasible-region.js)
         STACK: assets/stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/05-linear-programming/02-graphing-linear-inequalities/ -->

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 2.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-graphing-constraints">
        <title>Graphing Constraints: Shading the Unwanted Region</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> every constraint has a boundary line; the line is solid for <m>\le</m> and <m>\ge</m> and dashed for <m>\lt</m> and <m>\gt</m>; we shade the side that is not wanted, so the region R that satisfies every constraint is left clear.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> draw a boundary through two points, choose its style, use a test point to decide which side to shade, and label R with a key.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> graph the constraints of the class fundraiser and decide whether plans lie in R.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners graph the fundraiser model and see the region of possible plans left clear.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Give out graph paper and agree a scale of <m>1\,\text{cm}</m> to <m>10</m> items on both axes. After part (b), ask: <q>Which side did <m>(0, 0)</m> fall on, and did you shade it?</q></p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-graphing-the-fundraiser">
            <title>Graphing the Fundraiser</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The Grade 12 class in Machakos makes <m>x</m> mandazi and <m>y</m> samosas for its fundraiser (<xref ref="act-planning-the-fundraiser"/>). The constraints are
                    <md>x + y \le 100, \quad 15x + 35y \le 2520, \quad y \ge 30, \quad x \ge 0.</md>
                Draw axes with <m>x</m> from <m>0</m> to <m>170</m> and <m>y</m> from <m>0</m> to <m>110</m>.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Draw the line <m>x + y = 100</m> through <m>(100, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 100)</m>. Should it be solid or dashed?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Solid: <q>at most</q> allows exactly <m>100</m> items, so points on the line are allowed.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Test the point <m>(0, 0)</m> in <m>x + y \le 100</m>. Shade the side of the line where the inequality is false.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>0 + 0 = 0 \le 100</m> is true, so <m>(0, 0)</m> is on the wanted side. Shade the other side, above and to the right of the line.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find where <m>15x + 35y = 2520</m> crosses the axes. Draw it and shade its unwanted side in the same way.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>With <m>y = 0</m>, <m>x = 168</m>; with <m>x = 0</m>, <m>y = 72</m>. Draw the solid line through <m>(168, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 72)</m>. <m>(0, 0)</m> gives <m>0 \le 2520</m>, true, so shade the side away from the origin.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Draw <m>y = 30</m>. Which side do you shade?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>A solid horizontal line. The wanted side is <m>y \ge 30</m>, above the line, so shade below it.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Shade the side of the <m>y</m>-axis where <m>x \lt 0</m>. Label the region left clear R and add a key.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                                <p>R is the clear four-sided region shown in <xref ref="fig-fundraiser-region"/>. Its corners are found in <xref ref="act-corners-of-the-fundraiser-region"/>.</p>
                            </solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Is the plan <m>(50, 40)</m> in R? Is <m>(50, 55)</m>?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>(50, 40)</m>: <m>90 \le 100</m>, <m>750 + 1400 = 2150 \le 2520</m>, <m>40 \ge 30</m>: yes, it is in R. <m>(50, 55)</m>: <m>105 \gt 100</m>, so it lies in the shaded part: no.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To state the steps for graphing a constraint and the shading convention used in this chapter.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> after shading the unwanted side of every line, the clear part of the fundraiser graph is the set of possible plans.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Draw each boundary through two points, usually where it crosses the axes.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Solid for <m>\le</m> and <m>\ge</m>; dashed for <m>\lt</m> and <m>\gt</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Test a point that is not on the line, and shade the side where the inequality is false.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Label R and give a key every time.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-graphing-a-constraint">
            <title>Graphing a Constraint</title>
            <p>To graph a constraint such as <m>ax + by \le c</m>:
                <ol marker="1.">
                    <li><p>Draw the <term>boundary line</term> <m>ax + by = c</m> through two of its points, usually where it crosses the axes (put <m>y = 0</m>, then <m>x = 0</m>).</p></li>
                    <li><p>Draw the line solid for <m>\le</m> or <m>\ge</m>, because points on it satisfy the constraint, and dashed for <m>\lt</m> or <m>\gt</m>, because they do not.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Choose a test point that is not on the line: <m>(0, 0)</m>, or <m>(1, 0)</m> or <m>(0, 1)</m> if the line passes through the origin. Substitute it into the inequality.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Shade the side of the line where the inequality is false: the side that is not wanted.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            <p>Do this for every constraint. Shade what you cannot have, and what is left is what you can: the clear region satisfies every constraint at once. It is the required region of a set of inequalities, called the <term>feasible region</term> in linear programming, and we label it R. Every graph has a key: <q>Shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.</q></p>
        </insight>

        <p xml:id="p-shading-change-core" component="core">In Grade 11 you shaded the region you wanted. With three or more inequalities the shading piles up and the wanted region is hard to see, so from now on we shade what we do not want and leave R clear, as Kenyan examination papers usually do. Always say in a key which you have done.</p>
        <p xml:id="p-shading-recap-essential" component="essential">As in <xref ref="ch-linear-inequalities"/>, we shade the unwanted side of each line and leave R clear.</p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-lp-feasible-region">
            <caption>Drag the sliders to change each constraint and use the buttons to change its sign. The unwanted side of each line is shaded; the clear region R satisfies every constraint.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-lp-feasible-region" platform="jsxgraph" width="100%" aspect="16:9" source="jsxgraph/lp-feasible-region.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-lp-feasible-region" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:9"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>Each constraint is <m>ax + by \le c</m> or <m>ax + by \ge c</m>. Move its <alert>a</alert>, <alert>b</alert> and <alert>c</alert> sliders and watch its boundary line move. <m>x \ge 0</m> and <m>y \ge 0</m> always hold.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Press <alert>sign</alert> to switch between at most and at least, and see the shading jump to the other side. Press <alert>line</alert> to switch between a solid line (<m>\le</m>, <m>\ge</m>) and a dashed line (<m>\lt</m>, <m>\gt</m>).</p></li>
                            <li><p>R is outlined and its vertices are listed below the graph. Where two shaded parts overlap, the shading is darker.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Make R disappear by changing only the <alert>c</alert> slider of constraint 3. What is the smallest value that does it? What is left of R one step before?</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-graphing-one-constraint">
            <title>Graphing One Constraint</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Graph <m>5x + 6y \le 30</m> and shade the unwanted side.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The boundary is <m>5x + 6y = 30</m>. With <m>y = 0</m>, <m>x = 6</m>; with <m>x = 0</m>, <m>y = 5</m>. Draw the line through <m>(6, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 5)</m>, solid, since the sign is <m>\le</m>.</p>
                <p>Test <m>(0, 0)</m>: <m>5(0) + 6(0) = 0 \le 30</m> is true, so the origin is on the wanted side. Shade the side away from the origin.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-one-constraint">
                    <caption>The constraint <m>5x + 6y \le 30</m>: the side not containing the origin is shaded.</caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>The solid line 5x + 6y = 30 through (6, 0) and (0, 5); the side not containing the origin is shaded.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, axis line style={->}, grid=major, grid style={gray!25}, xlabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:1,0)}, yshift=-14pt}, ylabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:0,1)}, xshift=-4pt, rotate=90}, tick label style={font=\scriptsize}, clip=false, width=8cm, height=6.5cm, xmin=-1, xmax=9, ymin=-1, ymax=7, xtick={1,...,8}, ytick={1,...,6}, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}]
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:7.2,-1) -- (axis cs:9,-1) -- (axis cs:9,7) -- (axis cs:-1,7) -- (axis cs:-1,5.8333) -- cycle;
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-1,5.8333) -- (axis cs:7.2,-1);
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:6,0) {$(6, 0)$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:0,5) {$(0, 5)$};
\fill (axis cs:6,0) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:0,5) circle[radius=2pt];
\node[anchor=north east, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:0,0) {$O$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:4.2,3.4) {$5x + 6y = 30$};
\node[font=\large] at (axis cs:2,1.5) {$R$};
\end{axis}
\coordinate (k) at ([yshift=-0.2cm]current axis.outer south west);
\fill[gray!30, draw=black, thin] (k) rectangle ++(0.45,-0.3);
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at ([xshift=0.55cm, yshift=-0.15cm]k) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-boundary-through-origin">
            <title>A Boundary Through the Origin</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The hotel of <xref ref="example-hotel-chicken-and-beef"/> decides it wants more than three times as much chicken as beef: <m>y \gt 3x</m>. Graph this constraint.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The boundary <m>y = 3x</m> passes through <m>(0, 0)</m> and <m>(2, 6)</m>. Draw it dashed, since the sign is <m>\gt</m>: an order with exactly three times as much chicken is not allowed.</p>
                <p>The origin is on the line, so it cannot be the test point. Test <m>(1, 0)</m>: is <m>0 \gt 3 \times 1</m>? No, it is false. So <m>(1, 0)</m> is on the unwanted side: shade the side of the line containing <m>(1, 0)</m>, below and to the right of it.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-through-the-origin">
                    <caption>The constraint <m>y \gt 3x</m>: a dashed boundary through the origin, with the side containing <m>(1, 0)</m> shaded.</caption>
                    <image width="45%">
                        <shortdescription>The dashed line y = 3x through (0, 0) and (2, 6). The side containing the test point (1, 0), below and to the right of the line, is shaded.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, axis line style={->}, grid=major, grid style={gray!25}, xlabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:1,0)}, yshift=-14pt}, ylabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:0,1)}, xshift=-4pt, rotate=90}, tick label style={font=\scriptsize}, clip=false, width=6.5cm, height=7.5cm, xmin=-1, xmax=4, ymin=-2, ymax=10, xtick={1,2,3}, ytick={2,4,6,8}, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}]
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:-0.6667,-2) -- (axis cs:4,-2) -- (axis cs:4,10) -- (axis cs:3.3333,10) -- cycle;
\draw[thick, dashed] (axis cs:-0.6667,-2) -- (axis cs:3.3333,10);
\fill (axis cs:0,0) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:2,6) circle[radius=2pt];
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:2,6) {$(2, 6)$};
\draw (axis cs:1,0) circle[radius=3pt];
\node[anchor=north west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:1,0) {$(1, 0)$};
\node[anchor=south east, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:2.7,8.1) {$y = 3x$};
\node[font=\large] at (axis cs:0.6,6) {$R$};
\node[anchor=north east, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:0,0) {$O$};
\end{axis}
\coordinate (k) at ([yshift=-0.2cm]current axis.outer south west);
\fill[gray!30, draw=black, thin] (k) rectangle ++(0.45,-0.3);
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at ([xshift=0.55cm, yshift=-0.15cm]k) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-seedlings-region">
            <title>The Seedlings Region</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Graph the constraints of <xref ref="example-seedlings"/>: <m>50x + 120y \le 6000</m>, <m>x \ge 20</m>, <m>x + y \le 80</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>, where <m>x</m> is the number of grevillea and <m>y</m> the number of avocado seedlings. Label R.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>50x + 120y = 6000</m> passes through <m>(120, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 50)</m>; <m>(0, 0)</m> satisfies <m>0 \le 6000</m>, so shade away from the origin. <m>x + y = 80</m> passes through <m>(80, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 80)</m>; shade away from the origin. <m>x = 20</m> is a vertical line; the wanted side is to its right, so shade the left. <m>y = 0</m> is the <m>x</m>-axis; shade below it. All four lines are solid.</p>
                <p>R is the clear region. Its corners are <m>(20, 0)</m>, <m>(80, 0)</m>, about <m>(51.43, 28.57)</m> and about <m>(20, 41.67)</m>; <xref ref="subsec-vertices-and-whole-number-points"/> shows how to find them exactly.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-seedlings-region">
                    <caption>The seedlings region R, bounded by four solid lines.</caption>
                    <image width="90%">
                        <shortdescription>Four solid boundaries: 50x + 120y = 6000, x = 20, x + y = 80 and the x-axis, each shaded on its unwanted side. R has corners (20, 0), (80, 0), about (51.4, 28.6) and (20, 41.7).</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Horizontal axis x, grevillea, from 0 to 130. Vertical axis y, avocado, from 0 to 90. Boundary 50x + 120y = 6000 through (120, 0) and (0, 50), shaded above. Boundary x + y = 80 through (80, 0) and (0, 80), shaded above. Boundary x = 20, shaded to its left. The region below the x-axis is shaded. R is the clear region with vertices (20, 0), (80, 0), (360/7, 200/7), about (51.43, 28.57), and (20, 125/3), about (20, 41.67). Key: shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.</p></description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, axis line style={->}, grid=major, grid style={gray!25}, xlabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:1,0)}, yshift=-14pt}, ylabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:0,1)}, xshift=-4pt, rotate=90}, tick label style={font=\scriptsize}, clip=false, width=12cm, height=8cm, xmin=-10, xmax=130, ymin=-10, ymax=90, xtick={20,40,...,120}, ytick={20,40,...,80}, xlabel={$x$ (grevillea)}, ylabel={$y$ (avocado)}]
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:130,-4.1667) -- (axis cs:130,90) -- (axis cs:-10,90) -- (axis cs:-10,54.1667) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:-10,-10) -- (axis cs:20,-10) -- (axis cs:20,90) -- (axis cs:-10,90) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:90,-10) -- (axis cs:130,-10) -- (axis cs:130,90) -- (axis cs:-10,90) -- (axis cs:-10,90) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:-10,-10) -- (axis cs:130,-10) -- (axis cs:130,0) -- (axis cs:-10,0) -- cycle;
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-10,54.1667) -- (axis cs:130,-4.1667);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:20,-10) -- (axis cs:20,90);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-10,90) -- (axis cs:90,-10);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-10,0) -- (axis cs:130,0);
\fill (axis cs:20,0) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:80,0) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:51.4286,28.5714) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:20,41.6667) circle[radius=2pt];
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:20,0) {$(20, 0)$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:80,0) {$(80, 0)$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:51.4286,28.5714) {$(51.43, 28.57)$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:20,41.6667) {$(20, 41.67)$};
\node[font=\large] at (axis cs:42,15) {$R$};
\node[anchor=west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:22,80) {$x = 20$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:4,76) {$x + y = 80$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:95,10) {$50x + 120y = 6000$};
\end{axis}
\coordinate (k) at ([yshift=-0.2cm]current axis.outer south west);
\fill[gray!30, draw=black, thin] (k) rectangle ++(0.45,-0.3);
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at ([xshift=0.55cm, yshift=-0.15cm]k) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <note xml:id="note-lp-graphing-software">
            <title>Graphing Software</title>
            <p>Free graphing software such as GeoGebra draws inequalities: type <c>5x+6y&lt;=30</c> and it shades a side of the line. Most programs shade the side that is wanted, the opposite of this chapter, so check which side it has shaded before you copy the picture, and say which in your key.</p>
        </note>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type <m>\le</m> as <c>&lt;=</c> and <m>\ge</m> as <c>>=</c>, for example <c>4x+9y&lt;=72</c>. Give intercepts as exact values, for example <c>4.5</c>.</p>

                <exercise xml:id="ex-drawing-a-constraint">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-drawing-a-constraint" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/05-linear-programming/02-graphing-linear-inequalities/Drawing-a-constraint.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-inequalities-from-a-graph">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-inequalities-from-a-graph" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/05-linear-programming/02-graphing-linear-inequalities/Inequalities-from-a-graph.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-graphing-constraints">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-graph-four-nine">
                <statement><p>Graph <m>4x + 9y \le 36</m>, shading the unwanted side.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>A solid line through <m>(9, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 4)</m>, shaded on the side away from the origin</p></answer>
                <solution>
                    <p>With <m>y = 0</m>, <m>x = 9</m>; with <m>x = 0</m>, <m>y = 4</m>. The line is solid (<m>\le</m>). <m>(0, 0)</m> gives <m>0 \le 36</m>, true, so shade the side away from the origin.</p>
                    <figure xml:id="fig-graph-four-nine">
                        <caption>The constraint <m>4x + 9y \le 36</m>.</caption>
                        <image width="55%">
                            <shortdescription>The solid line 4x + 9y = 36 through (9, 0) and (0, 4); the side away from the origin is shaded and the side containing the origin is labelled R.</shortdescription>
                            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, axis line style={->}, grid=major, grid style={gray!25}, xlabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:1,0)}, yshift=-14pt}, ylabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:0,1)}, xshift=-4pt, rotate=90}, tick label style={font=\scriptsize}, clip=false, width=8cm, height=5.5cm, xmin=-1, xmax=11, ymin=-1, ymax=6, xtick={1,...,10}, ytick={1,...,5}, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}]
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:11,-0.8889) -- (axis cs:11,6) -- (axis cs:-1,6) -- (axis cs:-1,4.4444) -- cycle;
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-1,4.4444) -- (axis cs:11,-0.8889);
\fill (axis cs:9,0) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:0,4) circle[radius=2pt];
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:9,0) {$(9, 0)$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:0,4) {$(0, 4)$};
\node[anchor=north east, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:0,0) {$O$};
\node[font=\large] at (axis cs:2.5,1.3) {$R$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:5.4,2.2) {$4x + 9y = 36$};
\end{axis}
\coordinate (k) at ([yshift=-0.2cm]current axis.outer south west);
\fill[gray!30, draw=black, thin] (k) rectangle ++(0.45,-0.3);
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at ([xshift=0.55cm, yshift=-0.15cm]k) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-graph-strict-two-nine">
                <statement><p>Graph <m>2x + 9y \gt 18</m>, shading the unwanted side.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>A dashed line through <m>(9, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 2)</m>, shaded on the side containing the origin</p></answer>
                <solution>
                    <p>With <m>y = 0</m>, <m>x = 9</m>; with <m>x = 0</m>, <m>y = 2</m>. The line is dashed (<m>\gt</m>). <m>(0, 0)</m> gives <m>0 \gt 18</m>, false, so the origin is on the unwanted side: shade the side containing the origin.</p>
                    <figure xml:id="fig-graph-strict-two-nine">
                        <caption>The constraint <m>2x + 9y \gt 18</m>.</caption>
                        <image width="55%">
                            <shortdescription>The dashed line 2x + 9y = 18 through (9, 0) and (0, 2); the side containing the origin is shaded and the other side is labelled R.</shortdescription>
                            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, axis line style={->}, grid=major, grid style={gray!25}, xlabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:1,0)}, yshift=-14pt}, ylabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:0,1)}, xshift=-4pt, rotate=90}, tick label style={font=\scriptsize}, clip=false, width=8cm, height=4.5cm, xmin=-1, xmax=11, ymin=-1, ymax=4, xtick={1,...,10}, ytick={1,2,3}, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}]
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:-1,-1) -- (axis cs:11,-1) -- (axis cs:11,-0.4444) -- (axis cs:-1,2.2222) -- cycle;
\draw[thick, dashed] (axis cs:-1,2.2222) -- (axis cs:11,-0.4444);
\fill (axis cs:9,0) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:0,2) circle[radius=2pt];
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:9,0) {$(9, 0)$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:0,2) {$(0, 2)$};
\node[anchor=north east, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:0,0) {$O$};
\node[font=\large] at (axis cs:6,2.6) {$R$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:3.2,1.3) {$2x + 9y = 18$};
\end{axis}
\coordinate (k) at ([yshift=-0.2cm]current axis.outer south west);
\fill[gray!30, draw=black, thin] (k) rectangle ++(0.45,-0.3);
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at ([xshift=0.55cm, yshift=-0.15cm]k) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-graph-region-r4">
                <statement><p>Draw the region R given by <m>8x + 7y \le 112</m>, <m>2x + 7y \le 70</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m> and <m>y \ge 0</m>. Label R.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Boundaries through <m>(14, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 16)</m>, and through <m>(35, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 10)</m>, both solid; R is the four-sided region next to the origin</p></answer>
                <solution>
                    <p><m>8x + 7y = 112</m> passes through <m>(14, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 16)</m>; <m>2x + 7y = 70</m> passes through <m>(35, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 10)</m>. Both are solid, and <m>(0, 0)</m> satisfies both, so shade the side of each away from the origin. Shade left of the <m>y</m>-axis and below the <m>x</m>-axis.</p>
                    <figure xml:id="fig-graph-region-r4">
                        <caption>The region R of <xref ref="ex-graph-region-r4"/>.</caption>
                        <image width="70%">
                            <shortdescription>Solid lines 8x + 7y = 112 and 2x + 7y = 70, each shaded on the side away from the origin, and the axes. R has corners (0, 0), (14, 0), (7, 8) and (0, 10).</shortdescription>
                            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, axis line style={->}, grid=major, grid style={gray!25}, xlabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:1,0)}, yshift=-14pt}, ylabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:0,1)}, xshift=-4pt, rotate=90}, tick label style={font=\scriptsize}, clip=false, width=10cm, height=6cm, xmin=-2, xmax=38, ymin=-2, ymax=18, xtick={5,10,...,35}, ytick={5,10,15}, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}]
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:15.75,-2) -- (axis cs:38,-2) -- (axis cs:38,18) -- (axis cs:-1.75,18) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:38,-0.8571) -- (axis cs:38,18) -- (axis cs:-2,18) -- (axis cs:-2,10.5714) -- cycle;
