\draw[thick] (axis cs:-2,0) -- (axis cs:38,0);
\fill (axis cs:0,0) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:14,0) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:7,8) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:0,10) circle[radius=2pt];
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:14,0) {$(14, 0)$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:7,8) {$(7, 8)$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:0,10) {$(0, 10)$};
\node[anchor=north east, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:0,0) {$O$};
\node[font=\large] at (axis cs:4,3.5) {$R$};
\node[anchor=west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:1.6,15) {$8x + 7y = 112$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:26,2.8) {$2x + 7y = 70$};
\end{axis}
\coordinate (k) at ([yshift=-0.2cm]current axis.outer south west);
\fill[gray!30, draw=black, thin] (k) rectangle ++(0.45,-0.3);
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at ([xshift=0.55cm, yshift=-0.15cm]k) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-fundraiser-points">
                <statement><p>On your graph from <xref ref="act-graphing-the-fundraiser"/>, is the plan of <m>60</m> mandazi and <m>35</m> samosas in R? Is the plan of <m>80</m> mandazi and <m>32</m> samosas? Check each answer by substituting.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>(60, 35)</m>: yes. <m>(80, 32)</m>: no</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>(60, 35)</m>: <m>95 \le 100</m>, <m>900 + 1225 = 2125 \le 2520</m>, <m>35 \ge 30</m>: in R. <m>(80, 32)</m>: <m>112 \gt 100</m>, so it lies in the shaded part above <m>x + y = 100</m>: not in R.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-empty-region">
                <title>Challenge: An Empty Region</title>
                <statement><p>Show that no point satisfies all of <m>6x + y \le 12</m>, <m>x + 7y \ge 90</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m> and <m>y \ge 0</m>. What does the graph look like?</p></statement>
                <hint><p>How large can <m>x + 7y</m> be when <m>6x + y \le 12</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>?</p></hint>
                <answer><p>The largest value of <m>x + 7y</m> is <m>84</m>, less than <m>90</m>; the whole graph is shaded.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>The first constraint with the axes gives the triangle with corners <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(2, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 12)</m>. At these corners <m>x + 7y = 0</m>, <m>2</m> and <m>84</m>, so <m>x + 7y</m> is at most <m>84</m> on the triangle. It can never reach <m>90</m>, so no point satisfies every constraint: after shading, nothing is left clear. A model with an empty region has no possible plan, so a condition must be relaxed.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-graphing-constraints">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give pre-drawn axes with a scale. Work through <xref ref="example-graphing-one-constraint"/> and <xref ref="example-boundary-through-origin"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-graph-four-nine"/> and <xref ref="ex-graph-strict-two-nine"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-graph-four-nine"/> to <xref ref="ex-fundraiser-points"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-empty-region"/>. Extension: change one number in <m>x + 7y \ge 90</m> so that R has exactly one point (answer: <m>x + 7y \ge 84</m>, which leaves only <m>(0, 12)</m>).</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 2.2 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-vertices-and-whole-number-points">
        <title>Vertices and Whole-Number Points</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> a vertex of R is a point where two boundary lines meet; a vertex on a dashed line is not in R; when <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> count things, only the whole-number points of R are possible plans.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find a vertex exactly by solving two boundary equations together; list the whole-number points of a small region; write the inequalities of a region from its graph.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> find the corners of the fundraiser and tailor regions and the possible plans of a livestock farmer.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find the corners of the fundraiser region, first by reading the graph and then by solving equations.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Let groups read the corners from their graphs first and compare readings. Then ask: <q>Why is reading <m>(49, 51)</m> off the graph not enough?</q> (Different groups read different values; only solving gives the exact point.)</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-corners-of-the-fundraiser-region">
            <title>Corners of the Fundraiser Region</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use your graph of the fundraiser region R from <xref ref="act-graphing-the-fundraiser"/>, or <xref ref="fig-fundraiser-region"/>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-fundraiser-region">
                    <caption>The fundraiser region R. Every boundary is solid; the unwanted side of each is shaded.</caption>
                    <image width="90%">
                        <shortdescription>Lines x + y = 100, 15x + 35y = 2520 and y = 30, all solid; the unwanted side of each is shaded, and so is the side of the y-axis where x is negative. R, left clear, has corners (0, 30), (70, 30), (49, 51) and (0, 72).</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Horizontal axis x, mandazi, from 0 to 170. Vertical axis y, samosas, from 0 to 110. Boundary x + y = 100, solid, through (100, 0) and (0, 100), shaded above. Boundary 15x + 35y = 2520, solid, through (168, 0) and (0, 72), shaded above. Boundary y = 30, solid, shaded below. The side of the y-axis with x less than 0 is shaded. R is the clear region with vertices (0, 30), (70, 30), (49, 51) and (0, 72). Key: shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.</p></description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, axis line style={->}, grid=major, grid style={gray!25}, xlabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:1,0)}, yshift=-14pt}, ylabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:0,1)}, xshift=-4pt, rotate=90}, tick label style={font=\scriptsize}, clip=false, width=12cm, height=8.5cm, xmin=-10, xmax=170, ymin=-10, ymax=110, xtick={0,20,...,160}, ytick={0,20,...,100}, minor xtick={10,30,...,170}, minor ytick={10,30,...,110}, minor grid style={gray!15}, grid=both, xlabel={$x$ (mandazi)}, ylabel={$y$ (samosas)}]
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:110,-10) -- (axis cs:170,-10) -- (axis cs:170,110) -- (axis cs:-10,110) -- (axis cs:-10,110) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:170,-0.8571) -- (axis cs:170,110) -- (axis cs:-10,110) -- (axis cs:-10,76.2857) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:-10,-10) -- (axis cs:170,-10) -- (axis cs:170,30) -- (axis cs:-10,30) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:-10,-10) -- (axis cs:0,-10) -- (axis cs:0,110) -- (axis cs:-10,110) -- cycle;
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-10,110) -- (axis cs:110,-10);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-10,76.2857) -- (axis cs:170,-0.8571);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-10,30) -- (axis cs:170,30);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:0,-10) -- (axis cs:0,110);
\fill (axis cs:0,30) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:70,30) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:49,51) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:0,72) circle[radius=2pt];
\node[font=\large] at (axis cs:25,45) {$R$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:112,-6) {$x + y = 100$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:125,18) {$15x + 35y = 2520$};
\node[anchor=south, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:140,30) {$y = 30$};
\end{axis}
\coordinate (k) at ([yshift=-0.2cm]current axis.outer south west);
\fill[gray!30, draw=black, thin] (k) rectangle ++(0.45,-0.3);
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at ([xshift=0.55cm, yshift=-0.15cm]k) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Read the coordinates of the four corners of R from your graph.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>About <m>(0, 30)</m>, <m>(70, 30)</m>, <m>(50, 50)</m> and <m>(0, 72)</m>; readings of the third corner vary from group to group.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>The corner near <m>(50, 50)</m> is where <m>x + y = 100</m> meets <m>15x + 35y = 2520</m>. Solve these two equations together.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>From the first, <m>x = 100 - y</m>. Then <m>15(100 - y) + 35y = 2520</m>, so <m>1500 + 20y = 2520</m>, <m>y = 51</m> and <m>x = 49</m>. The corner is <m>(49, 51)</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the other three corners exactly. Which two lines meet at each?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>x = 0</m> and <m>y = 30</m> meet at <m>(0, 30)</m>. <m>y = 30</m> and <m>x + y = 100</m> meet at <m>(70, 30)</m>. <m>x = 0</m> and <m>15x + 35y = 2520</m> meet at <m>(0, 72)</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Is each corner in R?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Yes: every boundary is solid, so points on the lines, and the corners where they meet, are allowed.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To define a vertex, show how to find it exactly, and mark the whole-number points of a region.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> solving in part (b) gave <m>(49, 51)</m> exactly, where reading the graph gave only an estimate.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A vertex is where two boundary lines meet: solve their equations together.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A vertex on a dashed line is not in R.</p></li>
                    <li><p>When the unknowns count things, mark the whole-number points of R as dots: these are the possible plans.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-vertices-and-whole-number-points">
            <title>Vertices and Whole-Number Points</title>
            <p>A corner of the feasible region R is called a <term>vertex</term> (plural <term>vertices</term>). Two boundary lines meet at a vertex, so solve their equations together, by substitution or elimination, to find it exactly. Reading a vertex off a graph gives only an estimate. Give vertices exactly, as fractions, or to <m>2</m> decimal places.</p>
            <p>A vertex on a dashed boundary is not in R, although points of R come as close to it as you like.</p>
            <p>When <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> count things, only the <term>whole-number points</term> of R (points whose coordinates are both whole numbers) are possible plans. On a graph we mark them as dots. To list them, take each whole-number value of <m>y</m> in turn and find the whole-number values of <m>x</m> that satisfy every constraint.</p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-tailor-vertices">
            <title>Vertices of the Tailor's Region</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Find the vertices of the tailor's region from <xref ref="act-the-tailors-week"/>: <m>3x + 7y \le 126</m>, <m>x + 6y \le 64</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>, where <m>x</m> is the number of shirts and <m>y</m> the number of dresses.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The two sloping boundaries meet where <m>3x + 7y = 126</m> and <m>x + 6y = 64</m>. From the second, <m>x = 64 - 6y</m>. Substituting, <m>3(64 - 6y) + 7y = 126</m>, so <m>192 - 11y = 126</m>, <m>y = 6</m> and <m>x = 28</m>. Check: <m>3(28) + 7(6) = 126</m> and <m>28 + 36 = 64</m>.</p>
                <p>On the <m>x</m>-axis the cloth line comes first: <m>3x = 126</m> gives <m>(42, 0)</m>, while the machine line meets the axis further out, at <m>(64, 0)</m>. On the <m>y</m>-axis the machine line comes first: <m>6y = 64</m> gives <m>\left(0, \tfrac{32}{3}\right) \approx (0, 10.67)</m>, while the cloth line meets it at <m>(0, 18)</m>.</p>
                <p>The vertices are <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(42, 0)</m>, <m>(28, 6)</m> and <m>\left(0, \tfrac{32}{3}\right)</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-tailor-region">
                    <caption>The tailor's region R with its four vertices.</caption>
                    <image width="80%">
                        <shortdescription>Solid lines 3x + 7y = 126 and x + 6y = 64 meet at (28, 6); the side of each away from the origin is shaded. R has corners (0, 0), (42, 0), (28, 6) and (0, 10.67).</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Horizontal axis x, shirts, from 0 to 70. Vertical axis y, dresses, from 0 to 20. Boundary 3x + 7y = 126, solid, through (42, 0) and (0, 18). Boundary x + 6y = 64, solid, through (64, 0) and (0, 10.67). The side of each away from the origin is shaded, and so are the parts with x or y negative. R is the clear region with vertices (0, 0), (42, 0), (28, 6) and (0, 32/3), about (0, 10.67). Key: shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.</p></description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, axis line style={->}, grid=major, grid style={gray!25}, xlabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:1,0)}, yshift=-14pt}, ylabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:0,1)}, xshift=-4pt, rotate=90}, tick label style={font=\scriptsize}, clip=false, width=11cm, height=7cm, xmin=-4, xmax=70, ymin=-2, ymax=20, xtick={10,20,...,60}, ytick={4,8,...,16}, xlabel={$x$ (shirts)}, ylabel={$y$ (dresses)}]
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:46.6667,-2) -- (axis cs:70,-2) -- (axis cs:70,20) -- (axis cs:-4,20) -- (axis cs:-4,19.7143) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:70,-1) -- (axis cs:70,20) -- (axis cs:-4,20) -- (axis cs:-4,11.3333) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:-4,-2) -- (axis cs:0,-2) -- (axis cs:0,20) -- (axis cs:-4,20) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:-4,-2) -- (axis cs:70,-2) -- (axis cs:70,0) -- (axis cs:-4,0) -- cycle;
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-4,19.7143) -- (axis cs:46.6667,-2);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-4,11.3333) -- (axis cs:70,-1);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:0,-2) -- (axis cs:0,20);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-4,0) -- (axis cs:70,0);
\fill (axis cs:0,0) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:42,0) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:28,6) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:0,10.6667) circle[radius=2pt];
\node[anchor=north east, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:0,0) {$(0, 0)$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:42,0) {$(42, 0)$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:28,6) {$(28, 6)$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:0,10.6667) {$(0, 10.67)$};
\node[font=\large] at (axis cs:12,4) {$R$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:4,16.5) {$3x + 7y = 126$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:56,1.4) {$x + 6y = 64$};
\end{axis}
\coordinate (k) at ([yshift=-0.2cm]current axis.outer south west);
\fill[gray!30, draw=black, thin] (k) rectangle ++(0.45,-0.3);
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at ([xshift=0.55cm, yshift=-0.15cm]k) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-goats-and-sheep">
            <title>Goats and Sheep</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A farmer in Kajiado has Ksh <m>35\,000</m> to buy goats at Ksh <m>6000</m> each and sheep at Ksh <m>5000</m> each. Her pen has <m>26</m> square metres of floor; a goat needs <m>2</m> square metres and a sheep <m>7</m>. She wants at least one of each. List every plan she could choose.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Let <m>x</m> be the number of goats and <m>y</m> the number of sheep she buys. Money: <m>6000x + 5000y \le 35\,000</m>, that is <m>6x + 5y \le 35</m>. Space: <m>2x + 7y \le 26</m>. Also <m>x \ge 1</m> and <m>y \ge 1</m>, whole numbers.</p>
                <p>Take each value of <m>y</m> in turn.
                    <ul>
                        <li><p><m>y = 1</m>: <m>6x \le 30</m> and <m>2x \le 19</m>, so <m>x \le 5</m>: <m>x = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>y = 2</m>: <m>6x \le 25</m> and <m>2x \le 12</m>, so <m>x \le 4</m>: <m>x = 1, 2, 3, 4</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>y = 3</m>: <m>6x \le 20</m> and <m>2x \le 5</m>, so <m>x \le 2</m>: <m>x = 1, 2</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>y = 4</m>: <m>2x \le -2</m>, impossible.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p>There are <m>11</m> plans: <m>(1, 1)</m>, <m>(2, 1)</m>, <m>(3, 1)</m>, <m>(4, 1)</m>, <m>(5, 1)</m>, <m>(1, 2)</m>, <m>(2, 2)</m>, <m>(3, 2)</m>, <m>(4, 2)</m>, <m>(1, 3)</m> and <m>(2, 3)</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-goats-and-sheep">
                    <caption>The farmer's region with its <m>11</m> whole-number points marked as dots.</caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>Solid lines 6x + 5y = 35, 2x + 7y = 26, x = 1 and y = 1 with their unwanted sides shaded. Eleven dots in the clear region: (1, 1) to (5, 1), (1, 2) to (4, 2), (1, 3) and (2, 3).</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, axis line style={->}, grid=major, grid style={gray!25}, xlabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:1,0)}, yshift=-14pt}, ylabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:0,1)}, xshift=-4pt, rotate=90}, tick label style={font=\scriptsize}, clip=false, width=9cm, height=7cm, xmin=-0.5, xmax=8, ymin=-0.5, ymax=6, xtick={1,...,7}, ytick={1,...,5}, xlabel={$x$ (goats)}, ylabel={$y$ (sheep)}]
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:6.25,-0.5) -- (axis cs:8,-0.5) -- (axis cs:8,6) -- (axis cs:0.8333,6) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:8,1.4286) -- (axis cs:8,6) -- (axis cs:-0.5,6) -- (axis cs:-0.5,3.8571) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:-0.5,-0.5) -- (axis cs:1,-0.5) -- (axis cs:1,6) -- (axis cs:-0.5,6) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:-0.5,-0.5) -- (axis cs:8,-0.5) -- (axis cs:8,1) -- (axis cs:-0.5,1) -- cycle;
\draw[thick] (axis cs:6.25,-0.5) -- (axis cs:0.8333,6);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-0.5,3.8571) -- (axis cs:8,1.4286);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:1,-0.5) -- (axis cs:1,6);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-0.5,1) -- (axis cs:8,1);
\fill (axis cs:1,1) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:1,2) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:1,3) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:2,1) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:2,2) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:2,3) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:3,1) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:3,2) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:4,1) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:4,2) circle[radius=2pt];
\fill (axis cs:5,1) circle[radius=2pt];
\node[anchor=west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:2.3,4.4) {$6x + 5y = 35$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:6.1,2.05) {$2x + 7y = 26$};
\node[anchor=west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:1,5.6) {$x = 1$};
\node[anchor=south, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:6.8,1) {$y = 1$};
\node[font=\large] at (axis cs:2.5,2.5) {$R$};
\end{axis}
\coordinate (k) at ([yshift=-0.2cm]current axis.outer south west);
\fill[gray!30, draw=black, thin] (k) rectangle ++(0.45,-0.3);
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at ([xshift=0.55cm, yshift=-0.15cm]k) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-reading-a-region">
            <title>Reading a Region</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Write the inequalities that describe the region R left clear in <xref ref="fig-reading-a-region"/>. Which of its corners are not in R?</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-reading-a-region">
                    <caption>A region R bounded by <m>5x + 6y = 60</m> (solid), <m>y = 1</m> (dashed), <m>x = 6</m> (solid) and the <m>y</m>-axis.</caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>Solid line 5x + 6y = 60 through (12, 0) and (0, 10), shaded above. Dashed line y = 1, shaded below. Solid line x = 6, shaded to the right. The side of the y-axis with x negative is shaded. R is the clear region with corners (0, 1), (6, 1), (6, 5) and (0, 10).</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, axis line style={->}, grid=major, grid style={gray!25}, xlabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:1,0)}, yshift=-14pt}, ylabel style={anchor=north east, at={(axis description cs:0,1)}, xshift=-4pt, rotate=90}, tick label style={font=\scriptsize}, clip=false, width=8.5cm, height=7cm, xmin=-1, xmax=13, ymin=-1, ymax=11, xtick={2,4,...,12}, ytick={2,4,...,10}, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}]
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:13,-0.8333) -- (axis cs:13,11) -- (axis cs:-1,11) -- (axis cs:-1,10.8333) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:-1,-1) -- (axis cs:13,-1) -- (axis cs:13,1) -- (axis cs:-1,1) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:6,-1) -- (axis cs:13,-1) -- (axis cs:13,11) -- (axis cs:6,11) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!30, fill opacity=0.75] (axis cs:-1,-1) -- (axis cs:0,-1) -- (axis cs:0,11) -- (axis cs:-1,11) -- cycle;
\draw[thick] (axis cs:-1,10.8333) -- (axis cs:13,-0.8333);
\draw[thick, dashed] (axis cs:-1,1) -- (axis cs:13,1);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:6,-1) -- (axis cs:6,11);
\draw[thick] (axis cs:0,-1) -- (axis cs:0,11);
\node[font=\large] at (axis cs:2.5,4) {$R$};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:7.5,3.9) {$5x + 6y = 60$};
\node[anchor=west, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:6,10.4) {$x = 6$};
\node[anchor=south, font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:12.2,1) {$y = 1$};
\node[anchor=north east, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:0,0) {$O$};
\end{axis}
\coordinate (k) at ([yshift=-0.2cm]current axis.outer south west);
\fill[gray!30, draw=black, thin] (k) rectangle ++(0.45,-0.3);
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at ([xshift=0.55cm, yshift=-0.15cm]k) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>R is on the origin's side of <m>5x + 6y = 60</m>, and the line is solid: <m>5x + 6y \le 60</m>. R is above <m>y = 1</m>, which is dashed: <m>y \gt 1</m>. R is to the left of <m>x = 6</m>, which is solid: <m>x \le 6</m>. R is to the right of the <m>y</m>-axis: <m>x \ge 0</m>.</p>
                <p>The corners <m>(0, 1)</m> and <m>(6, 1)</m> lie on the dashed line, so they are not in R. The corners <m>(6, 5)</m> and <m>(0, 10)</m> are in R.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type exact values, for example <c>11/2</c> or <c>5.5</c>, and whole numbers for whole-number points.</p>

                <exercise xml:id="ex-vertices-of-a-feasible-region">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-vertices-of-a-feasible-region" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/05-linear-programming/02-graphing-linear-inequalities/Vertices-of-a-feasible-region.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-whole-number-points-in-a-region">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-whole-number-points-in-a-region" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/05-linear-programming/02-graphing-linear-inequalities/Whole-number-points-in-a-region.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-vertices-and-points">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-vertices-r4">
                <statement><p>Find the vertices of the region R of <xref ref="ex-graph-region-r4"/>: <m>8x + 7y \le 112</m>, <m>2x + 7y \le 70</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(14, 0)</m>, <m>(7, 8)</m>, <m>(0, 10)</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Subtracting <m>2x + 7y = 70</m> from <m>8x + 7y = 112</m> gives <m>6x = 42</m>, so <m>x = 7</m> and <m>y = 8</m>. On the <m>x</m>-axis the first line comes first, at <m>(14, 0)</m>; on the <m>y</m>-axis the second comes first, at <m>(0, 10)</m>. The vertices are <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(14, 0)</m>, <m>(7, 8)</m> and <m>(0, 10)</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-vertices-r5">
                <statement><p>Find the vertices of the region <m>7x + 4y \le 84</m>, <m>x + 8y \le 64</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(12, 0)</m>, <m>(8, 7)</m>, <m>(0, 8)</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Double the first equation: <m>14x + 8y = 168</m>. Subtract <m>x + 8y = 64</m>: <m>13x = 104</m>, so <m>x = 8</m> and <m>y = 7</m>. The axis intercepts that bound R are <m>(12, 0)</m> on <m>7x + 4y = 84</m> and <m>(0, 8)</m> on <m>x + 8y = 64</m>. The vertices are <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(12, 0)</m>, <m>(8, 7)</m> and <m>(0, 8)</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-fraction-vertex">
                <statement><p>Find where the lines <m>4x + 9y = 72</m> and <m>7x + 2y = 42</m> meet. Give the coordinates as fractions and to <m>2</m> decimal places.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>\left(\tfrac{234}{55}, \tfrac{336}{55}\right) \approx (4.25, 6.11)</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Multiply the first equation by <m>2</m> and the second by <m>9</m>: <m>8x + 18y = 144</m> and <m>63x + 18y = 378</m>. Subtract: <m>55x = 234</m>, so <m>x = \tfrac{234}{55} \approx 4.25</m>. Then <m>9y = 72 - \tfrac{936}{55} = \tfrac{3024}{55}</m>, so <m>y = \tfrac{336}{55} \approx 6.11</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-list-whole-number-points">
                <statement><p>List the whole-number points of the region <m>x \ge 1</m>, <m>y \ge 1</m>, <m>2x + 7y \le 30</m>, <m>y \lt 3</m>. How many are there?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>19</m> points: <m>(1, 1)</m> to <m>(11, 1)</m> and <m>(1, 2)</m> to <m>(8, 2)</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>y \lt 3</m> leaves <m>y = 1</m> or <m>y = 2</m>. With <m>y = 1</m>: <m>2x \le 23</m>, so <m>x = 1</m> to <m>11</m>: <m>11</m> points. With <m>y = 2</m>: <m>2x \le 16</m>, so <m>x = 1</m> to <m>8</m>: <m>8</m> points. Altogether <m>19</m> points.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-vertex-on-a-dashed-line">
                <title>Challenge: A Vertex on a Dashed Line</title>
                <statement><p>In the fundraiser, the class changes <q>at least <m>30</m> samosas</q> to <q>more than <m>30</m> samosas</q>, so <m>y \ge 30</m> becomes <m>y \gt 30</m>. Which vertices of R are no longer in R? Can the class still make a plan close to them?</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Which boundary becomes dashed?</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>(0, 30)</m> and <m>(70, 30)</m>; yes, for example <m>(69, 31)</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The line <m>y = 30</m> becomes dashed, so its points, including the vertices <m>(0, 30)</m> and <m>(70, 30)</m>, are not in R. Whole-number plans just above the line still work: <m>(69, 31)</m> has <m>100</m> items, costs <m>1035 + 1085 = 2120 \le 2520</m> and has <m>31 \gt 30</m> samosas. The other two vertices are on solid lines and stay in R.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-vertices-and-points">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a solving template (write both equations, make one letter the subject, substitute, check in both). Work through <xref ref="example-tailor-vertices"/> and <xref ref="example-goats-and-sheep"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-vertices-r4"/> and <xref ref="ex-vertices-r5"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-vertices-r4"/> to <xref ref="ex-list-whole-number-points"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-vertex-on-a-dashed-line"/>. Extension: how many whole-number points has the triangle <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>, <m>2x + 7y \le 14</m>? (Answer: <m>13</m>: eight with <m>y = 0</m>, four with <m>y = 1</m>, and <m>(0, 2)</m>.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
